finding: src/extras/gltf/draco.ts:93-109 and every k.info pointer read: wasm32 pointers at or above 2 GiB come back from the i32 exports as negative JS numbers, and the entry never applies >>> 0. Once kernel memory passes 2 GiB (a hostile glTF can force this, since the entry admits a stream up to 0x7fffffff bytes and expected counts up to u32), alloc returns a valid high pointer. `!input` lets it through, and `Uint8Array.set` then throws an unnamed "RangeError: Offset should not be negative" and leaks the input. Reproduced: two k.alloc(0x7ff00000) calls returned 1114120 and then -2147418096; the next decodeDraco threw that RangeError. Output pointers from info() have the same flaw, and memory.slice with a negative start reads from the end of memory, so faces or attributes placed above 2 GiB would come back silently wrong. The new regression does not cover this: 0xffffffff fails at Layout::array (> isize::MAX), so it never reaches the allocator or a high pointer.
